<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barwick-in-Elmet</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Barwick-in-Elmet is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-yorkshire">West Yorkshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, east of Leeds. It keeps large earthworks of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hillfort, later reused for a Norman motte, and its name recalls the British kingdom of Elmet, which survived in the area until the early 7th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barwick-in-elmet.jpg" alt="Barwick-in-Elmet" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chemical Engineer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barwick_Maypole_cross_pubs_14_June_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Barwick-in-Elmet lies 7 miles (11 km) east of Leeds city centre, in the civil parish of Barwick in Elmet and Scholes. Its name comes from the Old English bere, barley, and wīc, a settlement or specialised farm, so a barley farm or a grange producing barley for the lord. It is first recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> of 1086 as Bereuuith; the form Berewyke in Elmet appears in 1329. Scholes-in-Elmet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sherburn-in-elmet">Sherburn-in-Elmet</a> keep the same suffix, which medieval records also give to Clifford, Micklefield and Saxton.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earthworks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> fort are centred on Wendel Hill near the village; the site later held a Norman motte-and-bailey castle and, much later, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> observation post. Some scholars believe the capital of the kingdom of Elmet lay at or near Barwick. A taxation survey of 1379 counted 197 adults in about 100 households, and by 1821 the parish had 1,481 inhabitants.</p><p>The manor of Barwick and Scholes was for a time owned by the Gascoigne family of Parlington and Lotherton. The first known school opened in 1720, and Morwick Hall was built in the 18th century for Edward Gray, Lord Mayor of Leeds in 1749 and 1768. From the late 17th century to the early 20th, many villagers worked in the mines of Garforth, Cross Gates and Whitkirk, and the Cross Gates to Wetherby railway served nearby Scholes from 1874 until 1965.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The parish church, a grade II* listed building, keeps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> and Norman stonework and a 14th-century chancel. A wooden maypole 86 feet (26 m) high, the tallest in the UK, stands at the junction of Main Street and the Cross and is lowered and raised again at a festival every three years. Arthur Wood named his 1924 maypole dance Barwick Green, later the theme tune of The Archers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Barwick-in-Elmet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It is remembered in place names such as Sherburn-in-Elmet and <b>Barwick-in-Elmet</b>, and a 6th-century memorial stone at Llanaelhaearn in Gwynedd names a man of Elmet.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-elmet">Kingdom of Elmet</a></li></ul></section>
